# macOS
.DS_Store

# Rust (server/)
server/target/
server/target-linux/

# Android (android/)
android/.gradle/
android/build/
android/app/build/
android/local.properties
android/keystore.properties
android/*.jks
android/play-service-account.json
# Android Studio's "Generate Signed App Bundle / APK" wizard writes to
# app/<flavor>/<buildType>/, NOT to app/build/ — so nothing above catches
# it. `*.apk`/`*.aab` below caught the binaries, which is why this went
# unnoticed: what escaped was the R8 mapping (73 MB, and a fresh 73 MB on
# every release build), output-metadata.json and the baseline-profile .dm
# blobs. Untrack what is already in the index with
#     git rm -r --cached android/app/nettrash/release
# — the mapping file is a build artefact and belongs in the Play Console
# upload (Play deobfuscates crash reports with it), not in git history.
#
# Named per flavour rather than globbed: `android/app/*/release/` would
# also swallow a future `android/app/src/release/` source set, and an
# ignored SOURCE directory is a far worse failure than an unignored
# artefact one.
android/app/standard/
android/app/nettrash/
*.apk
*.aab

# iOS (ios/)
ios/**/xcuserdata/
ios/build/
ios/DerivedData/

# Python (tools/)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editors
.idea/
.vscode/
android/app/src/nettrash/google-services.json

# Web (web/) — Trunk's output and the lock file this repo does not track for
# an application, the same rule nettrash.me follows.
web/dist/
web/target/
web/Cargo.lock

# Windows client (win/) — the .NET build output, and the board oracle's own Cargo output.
# `bin/` and `obj/` are per-project and there are several, so they are globbed; the lock file
# follows the same rule the other two Rust trees here follow (an application, not a library).
win/**/bin/
win/**/obj/
win/tools/board-oracle/target/
win/tools/board-oracle/Cargo.lock
